<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:56:35Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Senegal reinstated as AFCON 2025 winners</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c44f73257efa8b8911a4fee7da5edaf419364eeeacf32d73e1314080a03493da</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:55:41Z</updated><published>2026-11-24T21:14:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/senegal-reinstated-as-afcon-2025-winners--0d709dc4ea27.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-reinstated-as-afcon-2025-winners" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, Senegal were reinstated as 2025 Africa Cup of Nations winners after the sport's highest court upheld their appeal against being stripped of the title.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Senegal reinstated as 2025 Africa Cup of Nations winners</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f41fddff603ccb74b7878997032a353f2e2ec2d41268872e3c4865100ed9280b</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:56:20Z</updated><published>2026-11-24T16:16:03Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261124-senegal-reinstated-as-2025-africa-cup-of-nations-winners--c447fcadc019.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261124-senegal-reinstated-as-2025-africa-cup-of-nations-winners" /><summary type="html">Senegal ​were reinstated as 2025 Africa Cup of Nations winners ​on Tuesday after the Court of Arbitration for Sport upheld their appeal against being stripped of the title. Senegal contested the ​Confederation ‌of African Football's decision to declare ⁠Morocco champions, despite Senegal beating the hosts 1-0 after extra time in ‌January's final in Rabat. Morocco announced that it would “pursue all necessary legal procedures” in response to the decision.</summary></entry><entry><title>Rwanda's malaria cases on the rise: Climate change leads to more mosquitoes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8f4d760551e6dba4b53a8365731849375b16fc6b3f5f3c2dfad27fb5fbed8c25</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:56:32Z</updated><published>2026-11-24T13:52:59Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261124-rwanda-s-malaria-cases-on-the-rise-climate-change-leads-to-more-mosquitoes--e188f03d0672.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261124-rwanda-s-malaria-cases-on-the-rise-climate-change-leads-to-more-mosquitoes" /><summary type="html">In Rwanda, malaria cases are on the rise due to climate change. Higher temperatures and prolonged rainfall are creating more breeding grounds for mosquitoes. According to researchers, the risk of infection could increase by as much as 20 percent by 2100 in the Rift Valley. Our correspondent Aurore Bayoud reports on Rwanda's efforts to tackle the problem.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>Africa disappointed with COP31 outcome</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:38b26d75a319736835454d57e52f550f8d1d7947490de3a148b34b5bf85202b3</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:56:35Z</updated><published>2026-11-23T22:14:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa-disappointed-with-cop31-outcome--31fcf02ce8c7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa-disappointed-with-cop31-outcome" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: The COP31 climate summit in Turkey has failed to reach an agreement, exposing deep divisions over fossil fuels and climate finance. Also, DR Congo's Félix Tshisekedi and Kenya's William Ruto agree to restore relations and boost regional cooperation. And determined to help their children, parents in a South African township have transformed a discarded shipping container into a sensory room.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>How far will Ukraine war spread? Kyiv targets Russian vessels in Libya</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:29ea85369543cbbc1b6f73f285521eee782915a8ea89b0bf6b37bd97563199d3</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-23T19:41:45Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261123-how-far-will-ukraine-war-spread-kyiv-targets-russian-vessels-in-libya--ff327a037bc6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261123-how-far-will-ukraine-war-spread-kyiv-targets-russian-vessels-in-libya" /><summary type="html">Talk about a spillover effect. In the Libyan port of Benghazi, Ukraine is claiming responsibility for the sinkings first of a Russian warship and then a cargo vessel. That's on top of the sanctioned ship struck in the Baltic Sea that's since strayed into Finland's waters. Are these legitimate targets?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>Ukraine claims strikes on Russian ships off Libyan coast</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:03bb6d181057e4bb403bb3f6e64857d6b9e646c961f69a97c8a4677e1c973a38</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-23T14:36:26Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261123-ukraine-claims-strikes-on-russian-ships-off-libyan-coast--c6da19d54d8f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261123-ukraine-claims-strikes-on-russian-ships-off-libyan-coast" /><summary type="html">Ukraine claimed responsibility on Monday for attacks on two Russian vessels off the coast of Libya the previous day. Here is what we know about the operation, claimed by President Volodymyr Zelensky shortly after the United Nations called for an investigation into the strikes.</summary></entry><entry><title>Russian warship hit by Ukrainian drone at Benghazi naval base, Libyan officials say</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2d1e46dab5953c96a89060c2b85c7b911e9a6e40bc1c2d96d84f42e6b6b4defd</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-22T19:41:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-russian-warship-hit-by-suspected-ukrainian-drone-at-libya-s-benghazi-naval-base--176c628065fb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-russian-warship-hit-by-suspected-ukrainian-drone-at-libya-s-benghazi-naval-base" /><summary type="html">A Russian navy corvette docked at the Benghazi naval base ​in eastern Libya was hit on Sunday by a Ukrainian sea drone, according to local officials, hours before a cargo ship was also hit by a sea drone in a second attack. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky on Monday confirmed that his country's military was behind the strikes.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Drone strikes hit homes and UN building in Sudan's Khartoum</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5aa3c65bf9672e7a62ff244531e1ca45ee7694fb70ff691a284d290c26f00b13</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-22T16:38:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-drone-strikes-hit-homes-and-un-building-in-sudan-s-khartoum--8d694ed4036b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-drone-strikes-hit-homes-and-un-building-in-sudan-s-khartoum" /><summary type="html">Drone strikes damaged residential homes and a United Nations building in Sudan's capital Khartoum early Sunday, with the army accusing neighbouring Ethiopia of launching the attacks. The strikes came as drone warfare intensifies in the country's conflict, now in its fourth year.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: Tackling period poverty and stigma</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f9d092ac3bc79f50327facf894a3c0124cb7ba5ab80cf633d428230b80b1da3e</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-21T12:22:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tackling-period-poverty-and-stigma--4f0bded21225.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tackling-period-poverty-and-stigma" /><summary type="html">In Ethiopia, entrepreneur Hanan Ahmed is tackling period poverty and the stigma surrounding menstruation. By producing affordable reusable sanitary pads and raising awareness in schools, she hopes to make menstrual health more accessible and help girls stay in class. Report by Awa Diouf &amp; Guillaume Gougeon.</summary></entry><entry><title>Guinea bans Burkina Faso broadcaster, escalating diplomatic tensions</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b322eaee09a55f80d0b406ebdc60b18881974fc64b2548822dd6ae1e8ecbd9d4</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-20T21:53:35Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261120-guinea-bans-burkina-faso-broadcaster-escalating-diplomatic-tensions--8233ee4e0a4a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261120-guinea-bans-burkina-faso-broadcaster-escalating-diplomatic-tensions" /><summary type="html">Tensions rise between Guinea and Burkina Faso after Conakry bans Burkinabé broadcaster RTB, amid a diplomatic dispute over gold miners and a caricature of President Mamadi Doumbouya. Meanwhile, fighting erupts between Congolese forces and the Rwanda-backed FRN group in eastern DR Congo, despite peace commitments. Finally, in Ethiopia, one entrepreneur is tackling period poverty by breaking menstrual stigma and making reusable sanitary pads more accessible.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Kenyan marathon champion Peres Jepchirchir banned for doping</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3c8f4a94f53a1bc1706b4d87ad6f839cb941815614abffefc621b9b44f943a0a</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-19T21:38:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261119-kenyan-marathon-champion-banned-for-doping--086370835999.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261119-kenyan-marathon-champion-banned-for-doping" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Kenyan athletics superstar and reigning marathon world champion Peres Jepchirchir is banned for three years for doping. Also, South African judges leave the courtroom behind and tour a repatriation centre before handing down judgment in 33 emergency asylum applications. Plus Ghana's presidency has a new jet, but critics of the ruling party are not on board.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>US has 'unbalanced approach' on DR Congo deal, Rwanda's foreign minister says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a48bd61ead5be19d2c2b6316d60b4f99119f3f4d10d34433af54702c41786d29</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-19T17:07:09Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20261119-us-has-unbalanced-approach-on-dr-congo-deal-rwanda-s-foreign-minister-says--1be6d8a48925.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261119-us-has-unbalanced-approach-on-dr-congo-deal-rwanda-s-foreign-minister-says" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24, Rwandan Foreign Minister Olivier Nduhungirehe said the Trump administration had "an unbalanced approach" in implementing the DR Congo peace deal it brokered. He also said that the recent row over the Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie's top job was "manufactured in Paris and Kinshasa". Finally, he defended a deal for Rwanda to host failed asylum seekers from five European countries.</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria: States set to get their own police forces</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:189250f160ff5659d67cf0393f710c4f26c602dafa202709b26e4d5936a24e03</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-19T12:20:35Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/nigeria-states-set-to-get-their-own-police-forces--a538130a0e7f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/nigeria-states-set-to-get-their-own-police-forces" /><summary type="html">Nigeria has moved a step closer to letting its 36 states set up their own police forces. The National Assembly has sent a constitutional amendment bill to the president, as authorities seek state-run police to complement the federal force amid growing insecurity. Many states currently fund vigilante groups to fight armed groups like Boko Haram, but these groups are poorly trained and ill-equipped for the task. FRANCE 24's correspondent has the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Senegal: Pastef removes President Faye from its ranks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3ddfa1964a2315192f7917476b86bc1fd17263902150601aca52e9c1f1c29099</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-19T12:05:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/senegal-pastef-removes-president-faye-from-its-ranks--1b2e7fd11a4f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-pastef-removes-president-faye-from-its-ranks" /><summary type="html">In Senegal, Pastef, the majority party led by National Assembly president Ousmane Sonko, has made official the departure of President Bassirou Diomaye Faye. It is the latest episode in a split with his former Prime minister that was already largely complete, and it comes in the middle of an economic crisis.</summary></entry><entry><title>President Bassirou Diomaye Faye excluded from Senegal's ruling party</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37376797583c4d19d3a63631c8292e78e7af06cfac2b099d7f8cc24f1df2d9fb</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-18T21:21:28Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/bassirou-diomaye-faye-excluded-from-senegal-s-ruling-party--66c181d4c833.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/bassirou-diomaye-faye-excluded-from-senegal-s-ruling-party" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: The political divorce between Senegal's president and his powerful former prime minister and mentor is official. Also, all of Nigeria's states have shown willingness for a push to create their own police forces. Plus we see how in parts of the continent, conflict and a drop in funding have placed huge pressure on children's learning.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Chinese cars shake up Ivory Coast's auto industry</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:af13b52deb39b51b6ed698265d27c53ab70a35258e5109684617f1ae1b21709a</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-18T12:25:36Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261118-chinese-cars-shake-up-ivory-coast-s-auto-industry--5631a171e161.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261118-chinese-cars-shake-up-ivory-coast-s-auto-industry" /><summary type="html">In the space of just a few years, more than a dozen Chinese carmakers have established themselves in Ivory Coast and are becoming increasingly visible on the country's streets. How have they managed to win over a growing number of customers? FRANCE 24's Jean Barrère and Eunice Stolz-Masson report.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>AI imagery complicates Nigeria's election preparations</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:343b62d8fc9969dc486b82be5e0002a008e52763e816faf01e6399039c0153d2</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-17T21:20:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ai-imagery-complicates-nigeria-s-election-prep--1ee3eaa6f6dc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ai-imagery-complicates-nigeria-s-election-prep" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Concerns emerge that artificial intelligence has been used to generate fake voting profiles ahead of Nigeria's high-stakes general election in January. Also, Nigeria's mining marshal heads underground to unearth some of the people and organisations behind the vast illegal mining industries. Plus a groundbreaking archaeological discovery in northern Sudan could be the ancient site of the largest church ever built in one of medieval Nubia's most important Christian sites.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Predictable: DR Congo-Rwanda rivalry spills over to Francophonie summit</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d40de935fdbed4f569e228e7e76952b090fb763ef59c2d7116c38e17d3b740b6</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-16T22:29:45Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261116-la-francophonie-waning-in-global-influence-linguistic-institutions-becoming-less-important--12f156dde70a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261116-la-francophonie-waning-in-global-influence-linguistic-institutions-becoming-less-important" /><summary type="html">François Picard is pleased to welcome Phil Clark, professor of international politics at SOAS University of London. The diplomatic crisis between Rwanda and DR Congo spilling out at the Francophonie summit in Phnom Penh has exposed an unexpected shift in their relations. Clark says that "nobody expected Rwanda to back down in this way" following President Emmanuel Macron's request that President Paul Kagame withdraw Louise Mushikiwabo's candidacy for a third term as head of the group.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>Rwanda to host hub for rejected EU migrants</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:eb442f3bd3530cacc8b11eecccb4bc3eea1e8235ccc17b615c98e544a9cac7ad</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-16T21:21:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/rwanda-to-host-hub-for-rejected-eu-migrants--5f2ca0d474bb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/rwanda-to-host-hub-for-rejected-eu-migrants" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Five European countries are negotiating a deal to deport rejected asylum seekers to Rwanda. Also, DR Congo and Rwanda's political animosity spills over at the Francophonie Summit in Cambodia. Plus in Tunisia, young women are increasingly turning towards weightlifting and bodybuilding as a form of self-expression.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Francophonie summit postpones new leader vote amid DR Congo-Rwanda dispute</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9b5096341b3c139aef902929b0f1611e328228734e271ed8e5f1be07dcfde657</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-16T10:51:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-francophonie-summit-postpones-new-leader-vote-amid-dr-congo-rwanda-dispute--712dbf9f04b5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-francophonie-summit-postpones-new-leader-vote-amid-dr-congo-rwanda-dispute" /><summary type="html">The Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie (OIF) on Monday had been set to choose its new secretary general during talks in the Cambodian capital Phnom Penh. But delegates decided to postpone the election for a year after a walkout threat by the Democratic Republic of Congo amid an ongoing dispute between Kinshasa and Kigali.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Five EU nations unveil migrant deportation centre deal with Rwanda</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:04e3729ddfac19c52c3269dcf415cbbba4ea38a27570cba89b9c9d3c8b8ad8a7</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-16T06:07:22Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-eu-nations-to-unveil-rwanda-deal-for-migrant-return-hub--23127c00a204.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-eu-nations-to-unveil-rwanda-deal-for-migrant-return-hub" /><summary type="html">Five EU countries on Monday announced a deal to establish a migrant deportation centre in Rwanda – the first such facility outside the bloc. The move comes after the EU toughened its migration rules in late 2025.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Canaries urge government action after 2,200 migrants reach the islands in 48h</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2d90b9bc8b853d4459d9783aba68bfbc4e80faa235f269bb9aa70bea7ad4b82b</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-15T15:24:58Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261115-canaries-urge-government-action-after-2-200-migrants-reach-the-islands-in-48h-1--7c412d3109df.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261115-canaries-urge-government-action-after-2-200-migrants-reach-the-islands-in-48h-1" /><summary type="html">Spain says nearly 2,200 migrants have arrived by boat to the Canary Islands this weekend. The Spanish islands are one of the main entry points into Europe for migrants from Africa. The Spanish Interior Ministry says more than 13,000 arrived there between January and the end of last month. Details and analysis by Andrew Hilliar, FRANCE 24 international affairs editor.</summary></entry><entry><title>A deep dive into Ivory Coast's solar energy goals</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:206f23725c46e2784a8f2ea7f0dc40ae91530b7373f08f40f55f900bfc99c0a9</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-15T14:25:24Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/a-deep-dive-into-ivory-coast-s-solar-energy-goals--daefc9aa7c4c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/a-deep-dive-into-ivory-coast-s-solar-energy-goals" /><summary type="html">Can Ivory Coast, which remains heavily dependent on fossil fuels, turn to solar power to make its energy consumption greener and more sustainable? We visited the country's new solar power plant in Ferkessédougou, in the north of the country. Inaugurated in July, it is expected to generate nearly 90 gigawatt-hours of electricity a year. Report by Julia Guggenheim.</summary></entry><entry><title>Cape Verde's President José Maria Neves wins second term</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:068471c9d954d2e1620bf1cd18c3cf58d5696e4c73e82a4cc674434ba2bd081a</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-15T07:56:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20261115-cape-verde-could-make-election-history-with-first-female-president--3cb625736ad1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261115-cape-verde-could-make-election-history-with-first-female-president" /><summary type="html">Cape Verde's President Jose Maria Neves won re-election on Sunday with 63.8 percent of votes cast, provisional electoral commission results showed. Neves had been the favourite in the West African archipelago. His main challenger was Joana Rosa Amado, the country's first woman presidential candidate, who conceded and congratulated Neves Monday morning.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Youth Olympic Games organisers hail Senegal's capability to host major events</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:714ce43e205a002686c2564fe5ac27050dfe7b12195c6f67e0c0f91da1eb3bd2</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T17:25:51Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/youth-olympic-games-organisers-hail-senegal-s-capability-to-host-major-events--044ed43c0324.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/youth-olympic-games-organisers-hail-senegal-s-capability-to-host-major-events" /><summary type="html">Dakar 2026 is over. Two weeks of competition, thousands of young athletes and a historic first for Africa. The Youth Olympic Games brought the world to Senegal for the first Olympic sporting event ever held on the continent. From record-breaking performances to historic medals for the host nation, Dakar 2026 has been a celebration of sport, youth and cultural exchange. Our correspondent Aminatou Diallo reports from Dakar.</summary></entry><entry><title>DR Congo, Rwanda clash over Francophonie leadership as Macron seeks compromise</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:047966d095442c18694d890ffd03224d2713f738e8d6610b638df2a0f374712d</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T14:54:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20261114-dr-congo-rwanda-clash-over-francophonie-leadership-as-macron-seeks-compromise--8dd812a22403.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261114-dr-congo-rwanda-clash-over-francophonie-leadership-as-macron-seeks-compromise" /><summary type="html">DR Congo and Rwanda clashed Saturday over the leadership of the Francophonie, as tensions between the neighbouring countries spilled into the race to head the 53-member organisation. French President Emmanuel Macron urged Rwanda to withdraw its candidate in favour of a compromise choice ahead of Monday's vote.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Inside Ethiopia's war: Rare access to a hospital treating victims of renewed fighting</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5d36b8254555d43f7b1f81790403e693e7aaf0f44da3035418205d511080cf16</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T12:23:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/inside-ethiopia-s-war-rare-access-to-a-hospital-treating-victims-of-renewed-fighting--64d2eb5d22c0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/inside-ethiopia-s-war-rare-access-to-a-hospital-treating-victims-of-renewed-fighting" /><summary type="html">Ethiopia's renewed conflict is taking an increasing toll on civilians, with humanitarian organisations reporting a rise in casualties. In the Amhara region, FRANCE 24’s correspondent Tom Canetti was granted rare access to a civilian hospital where surgeons are struggling to treat the influx of wounded patients. But for many caught in the fighting, reaching medical care is a challenge in itself.
 
Warning: The report contains images of surgery that may be confronting for some viewers.</summary></entry><entry><title>Gambia's Adama Barrow seeks third term</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9e771e385b690fd72d103e4d2ad470bf1dafe003a777ecb906596e4835238f52</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T21:46:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/gambia-s-adama-barrow-seeks-third-term--167d5d5b0803.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/gambia-s-adama-barrow-seeks-third-term" /><summary type="html">In tonight's programme, Gambia's electoral commission has announced five official candidates for the country's upcoming presidential election including incumbent Adama Barrow who is seeking a third term. Tensions continue to rise in Ethiopia following the federal government's appointment of a new head of the Western Tigray region. And the Dakar Youth Olympic Games come to a close after two weeks of intense competition.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Guinea opens illegal gold mining trial, 1,200 people arrested</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ff96c8d7c17ddf9529f5d9a0430867ec0b880d14047ebbe8bb5b3d7f71a27a62</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T10:28:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-guinea-opens-illegal-gold-mining-trial-1-200-people-arrested--3d42129ab965.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-guinea-opens-illegal-gold-mining-trial-1-200-people-arrested" /><summary type="html">Guinea opened a trial over illegal gold mining on Thursday, November 12, after a crackdown on mineral extraction in the district of Siguiri. There was a ban on "unregulated and illegal" gold mining in the area where the military was subsequently deployed. Security forces have expelled tens of thousands of foreign nationals who had come to work in the region's mines.</summary></entry><entry><title>Toera's homecoming: Madagascar's slain king joins his ancestors after 129 years</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d55261499b6fd96f31640d57fc85246793c508ef12168726c1038ab410e2d4d3</id><updated>2026-11-25T00:54:25Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T10:27:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-toera-s-homecoming-madagascar-s-slain-king-joins-his-ancestors-after-129-years--6b1fca90b490.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261113-toera-s-homecoming-madagascar-s-slain-king-joins-his-ancestors-after-129-years" /><summary type="html">In 1897, French colonial troops killed King Toera, ruler of Madagascar's powerful Sakalava kingdom of Menabe, and took his skull to Paris, where it remained hidden in a museum for more than a century. France returned the remains last year, and for the first time since his death, King Toera took his place among his ancestors during the Sakalava people's most sacred ritual.</summary></entry></feed>
